Track 2: The Slate Takes the Tom’s Place

Story & Context: Ife builds the EP’s most asymmetric groove at 132 BPM from a five-hit low-percussion cycle spread across two bars. Owain duplicates the pattern on tenor rototoms, then begins replacing one tuned drum hit every fourth cycle with a struck slate offcut. Because the slate has almost no low fundamental, each substitution creates a sudden loss of mass while preserving timing.

Lowri plays short crwth double stops alternating between open fifth and compressed minor seventh. Mara answers the more dissonant interval with a single prepared clavichord note rather than a chord. Nia uses plunger-muted low falls only after slate substitutions, making each lighter percussion event cast a darker brass shadow.

Halfway through, Ife removes the electronic kick completely for eight bars. Rototoms, slate, bridge-contact knocks, and crwth bow pulses carry the rhythm. When the kick returns it does not coincide with the slate; the two materials continue alternating as separate forms of weight.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Overhead view of dark slate slabs arranged beside two tenor rototoms on an old loading floor, one slate piece occupying a chalk circle labeled “TOM,” crwth bridge and muted trombone bell at opposite edges of frame. Spoken fragments limited to “wood to skin,” “skin to stone,” “leave the mass out,” and “same place, different weight.”

Track 3: Pressure Falls After the Wagon Clears

Story & Context: The closer begins at 124 BPM with contact-mic field audio of a narrow-gauge wagon wheel rolling across an embedded rail joint. Ife edits only the joint impact, not the full wheel sound, and places it once every eight bars. Lowri sustains a dark Aeolian drone while gradually reducing bow pressure instead of increasing it. The structural cue is therefore release: when the bridge signal falls below the marked threshold, Nia enters with a muted bass-trombone pedal and Mara strikes one low clavichord dyad.

Owain uses rototom rolls only in the spaces before pressure release, stopping the instant Lowri’s bow becomes light. The effect reverses the genre’s normal logic: dense percussion predicts silence rather than climax. In the final minute, each instrument leaves after one complete release cycle until only crwth remains.

Lowri finishes with an open drone so lightly bowed that the contact microphone almost stops registering while the room microphone still hears tone. The final sound is a loose wagon chain settling against the floor after everyone has stopped playing.

